    "title": "Hypothetical: What constitutes evidence for local LLM privacy improvements?",
    "body": "This post explores a hypothetical scenario for evaluating privacy benefits of local Large Language Model (LLM) deployments. The core question is: what observable evidence would suggest that running an LLM locally genuinely enhances user privacy compared to cloud-based services?\n\nIn this hypothetical setup, the primary consideration is that all prompts remain on the local machine, never transmitted to external servers. This isolation is a foundational element. However, to confirm a privacy improvement, we must also consider other factors. Specifically, a thorough hypothetical evaluation would need to examine local logging practices to ensure no sensitive data is inadvertently stored or accessible. Furthermore, any optional network connections, even for updates or diagnostics, would require scrutiny to verify they do not exfiltrate prompt data or model interactions.\n\nCrucially, this remains a theoretical discussion. No actual security audits or compliance certifications have been performed on this hypothetical system (F1, F2, F3). What specific metrics or checks would you consider definitive evidence of privacy enhancement in such a local deployment?",
