    "title": "Assessing Local LLM Privacy: A Hypothetical Framework",
    "body": "Considering a hypothetical scenario, how can we rigorously define and identify evidence that local deployment of Large Language Models (LLMs) leads to improved privacy? This is a crucial question for users prioritizing data security.\n\nThe premise is that the LLM operates entirely on the user's machine, meaning prompts are processed locally (F1). While this inherently reduces exposure compared to cloud services, it's not sufficient on its own. To establish a stronger case for privacy improvement, the hypothetical evaluation must extend to the system's behavior beyond prompt handling. This includes verifying that local logs do not retain sensitive information and that any configured network activity, even if optional, is strictly controlled and does not leak user data (F2).\n\nIt is important to reiterate that this is a hypothetical framework for discussion. No formal security audits or compliance certifications have been conducted within this conceptual model (F3). What additional observable indicators or verification steps would be necessary to confidently assert that a local LLM deployment offers a tangible privacy advantage?",
    "fact_refs": ["F1", "F2", "F3"],
    "plan": "Outline a hypothetical framework for assessing local LLM privacy. Emphasize prompt isolation, log verification, and network activity checks as key evidence points. Clearly state the hypothetical nature and absence of formal audits. Solicit input on further necessary verification steps."
